{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/intersectional-stereotype",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "intersectional stereotype"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "Stereotypes that arise from the convergence of several grounds – for example sex or gender, disability, ethnic origin, age or sexual orientation – and that generate a specific preconception, distinct from the mere sum of each ground taken separately."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "In the case of women with disabilities, they are often ascribed traits such as asexuality, the supposed inability to mother, or dependency, thereby compounding discrimination and making it harder to remedy when each ground is examined in isolation. International law requires combating them: the CRPD recognises women and girls with disabilities as a group exposed to multiple forms of discrimination (art. 6) and requires action against stereotypes, prejudices and harmful practices affecting persons with disabilities (art. 8)."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "6.10.2"
  ],
  "broader": {
    "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/stereotypes",
    "prefLabel": {
      "en": "stereotypes"
    }
  },
